# The build context for the enterprise control plane image.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL, WHICH IS NOT A PACKAGING PREFERENCE. The
# repository root .dockerignore excludes ee/, and ee/README.md names that
# exclusion as the fourth of the four mechanisms that keep enterprise code out
# of the community artifact: "`.dockerignore` excludes `ee` from the image build
# context, so the published control plane image is built from a context that
# does not contain it." That sentence has to stay true. Deleting the line to let
# this image build would trade a real edition boundary for a convenience, and it
# would do it silently, because nothing downstream would go red.
#
# BuildKit reads `<dockerfile>.dockerignore` from beside the Dockerfile and uses
# it INSTEAD OF the root .dockerignore for that Dockerfile alone. So the
# community image keeps a context that cannot see ee/, this image gets one that
# can, and neither list is a compromise between the two.
#
# IF THIS FILE IS EVER NOT READ, THE BUILD FAILS LOUDLY RATHER THAN WRONGLY.
# The classic pre-BuildKit builder does not support a per-Dockerfile ignore
# file. Under it the root list applies, ee/ is absent from the context, and
# `COPY ee/web/package.json` fails with a missing file. That is the correct
# failure mode and it is worth knowing about: this cannot degrade into an image
# that quietly ships without the enterprise tree, because every enterprise path
# in the Dockerfile is a COPY and a COPY of nothing is an error.
#
# The list below is the root one, with three deliberate differences, each
# marked. Everything else is kept in step with it on purpose: a context that
# drifts is how the sibling image once tarred up six hundred megabytes of
# marketing site and took thirty minutes instead of forty seconds.
.git
.github
engine
runner
tools
docs
examples
schemas
bin
infra
www
deploy
# DIFFERENCE 1. The Dockerfiles have to survive the exclusion above, the same
# way they do in the root list: `af up` sends the context to the daemon and
# names the Dockerfile inside it, so an excluded one is simply not there.
!deploy/docker/control-plane-enterprise.Dockerfile
!deploy/docker/bootstrap.mjs
!deploy/docker/maintenance.mjs
!deploy/docker/af-operator
!deploy/docker/personas.mjs
# DIFFERENCE 2. ee/ is NOT excluded here, which is the entire reason this file
# exists. What is excluded from it is the same class of thing excluded from
# web/: the Go module, which this image does not run, the tests, and the
# installed and generated trees, which the image builds from the lockfile.
ee/engine
ee/web/node_modules
ee/web/**/node_modules
ee/web/**/test
web/node_modules
web/**/node_modules
web/**/.next
web/**/test
**/*.test.ts
.antifailure
.gate-reports
.playwright-mcp
**/*.broken.*
console/node_modules
console/.next
console/out
**/.env
**/.env.*
# DIFFERENCE 3, and it is a difference in effect rather than in the line. A
# single `*` in an ignore pattern does not cross a `/`, so `*.md` is markdown at
# the top level only and ee/LICENSE.md reaches the context on its own. The
# negation is written anyway, because that is a property of Docker's matcher
# rather than of anything visible here, and the cost of a reader getting it
# wrong is an image that ships without the license it is covered by. This image
# is not MIT, and the license belongs inside it rather than in a registry label
# alone.
*.md
!ee/LICENSE.md
antifailure.yaml
masking.yaml
